<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Polyrrhenia</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.) · capital Polyrrhenia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Fortified hill city of western Crete, the leading power of the island's far west in the Hellenistic period.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Polyrrhenia stood on a steep hill south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-kissamos">Gulf of Kissamos</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> says it was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a> and Laconians, who united the scattered villages of the area. Its name, meaning rich in flocks, reflects its pastoral economy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Polyrrhenia controlled the west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, with harbours at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalasarna">Phalasarna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kissamos">Kisamos</a> at times under its influence. It had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosmoi">kosmoi</a> magistrates like other Cretan cities. In the Lyttian War of 220 BCE it broke with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/knossos">Knossos</a> and allied with Philip V of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia</a>. Its walls and cisterns survive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Polyrrhenia came under Roman rule in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 69 to 67 BCE and continued as a town, later a Byzantine bishopric.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/polyrrhenia.jpg" alt="Polyrrhenia" width="320" height="202"><figcaption>Image: Pampuco · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Polyrrinia_acropolis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 67 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Polyrrhenia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cretan Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Polyrrhenion</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=polyrrhenia" aria-label="Polyrrhenia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5278 896 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Polyrrhenia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5598 1152L5632 1166M5598 1152L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5632 1166h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5598 1152h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5598 1152h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-384&amp;focus=polyrrhenia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

